    fidelity: {
      "level": "behavioral",
      "areas": {
        "logic": {
          "level": "ok",
          "note": "Runs the real compiled firmware in Renode (an open-source emulator, MIT) in a local Docker container, in lockstep with Jumper's clock. GPIO (with pin interrupts), Serial, Wire and SPI reach Jumper's parts through Jumper's bridge (every signal time-stamped to the instruction)."
        },
        "timing": {
          "level": "approx",
          "note": "Virtual time counts one instruction per core clock cycle at the board's clock (no pipeline, wait-state or cache timing), so delay(), millis() and timers are exact in virtual time and busy loops are close. A part's reaction to a pin edge lands at the next simulation step (1 ms by default), not mid-step. Measured on an M4 Max: about 0.5 to 1x real time with 1 ms steps (busy-wait delay() runs every instruction)."
        },
        "analog": {
          "level": "approx",
          "note": "Pins are ideal sources with about 50 Ω output resistance and 40 kΩ pull-ups; inputs switch at 0.3 / 0.7 x 3.3 V. analogRead on ADC1's pins converts the solved net voltage to 12 bits against an ideal 3.3 V reference at once (no sampling time, noise or non-linearity)."
        },
        "power": {
          "level": "approx",
          "note": "USB (power.usb) puts 5 V on the 5V pin; the on-board LDO makes 3V3 from it (dropout typical). The board draws its typical current; per-pin current above the limit is flagged. Brownout and sleep currents are not simulated."
        },
        "radio": {
          "level": "n/a",
          "note": "No radio on this board."
        }
      },
      "emulates": [
        "STM32F401RE Cortex-M core and the peripherals Arduino sketches use, in Renode 1.17 (jumper/renode image)",
        "Digital I/O with pull-ups and pin interrupts (attachInterrupt)",
        "Serial (the board's console UART) with per-byte virtual time",
        "Wire (I2C master) to the I2C parts on the board's default pins",
        "SPI (master) to the SPI parts on the board's default pins",
        "analogRead on ADC1 channels (Jumper's ADC model / Renode's STM32 ADC fed by Jumper)",
        "analogWrite / Servo / tone through the timers' PWM outputs where Renode routes them to the pin",
        "DWT cycle counter (delayMicroseconds)",
        "ADC1 through Renode's STM32 ADC model, fed by Jumper"
      ],
      "doesNotEmulate": [
        "Other UARTs than the console (Serial2, Serial3) reaching parts",
        "Wire / SPI on other than the default pins (Wire.setSDA, SPI.setMOSI)",
        "USB (the board's USB device: USB CDC Serial, HID)",
        "DMA-driven transfers to parts",
        "Flash / EEPROM emulation persisting across a reset",
        "Low-power modes and their current",
        "The reset button and the NRST pin",
        "The RTC on its 32.768 kHz crystal and VBAT",
        "Bus electrical detail (clock stretching, rise times)",
        "The ST-LINK (USB mass storage, virtual COM port as a USB device): Serial is shown in Jumper's serial monitor instead",
        "The morpho headers (only the Arduino headers and PC13 are drawn)",
        "B1 as a control on the board (wire a pushbutton to PC13)",
        "Pin interrupts (attachInterrupt): they never fire on this board in Renode (tried PA8, PA10, PB5; cause not found; the Black Pill F411 on the same platform works). The board stays planned until this works"
      ],
      "testOnRealDevice": [
        "Upload: DFU / ST-LINK / serial bootloader on your computer",
        "Analog readings: the real ADC has offset, noise and sampling-time effects",
        "Timing-critical code: the emulator counts one cycle per instruction",
        "5 V parts: only the FT pins tolerate 5 V as inputs; outputs are 3.3 V",
        "I2C scanners: on these boards each absent address costs about 130 ms of virtual time in the emulator (Renode's STM32 I2C model plus STM32duino's timeout), so a full scan is slow; addressed devices answer at full speed"
      ]
    },
